FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1712-1739 — the `extends` edge is attached from **source order**, not from whether the parent object is actually declared (and in scope) when the child's `object` statement executes. The guard only checks `bindings[parent].location < bindings[child].location` and `kind == Object`; it never checks that the parent binding is visible at the child token. So `to make()` / `object Person ... end` / `end` followed by a top-level `object Employee extends Person` attaches a parent edge even though the runtime (`declare_object`, src/interpreter.rs:2993-3007) looks the parent up in `context.objects` at declaration time and would report `Object 'Employee' extends 'Person', which is not declared` because `make` was never called. Navigation then resolves `Employee.age` to a field the program can never reach. The same applies to a parent declared inside a not-taken `if`/`unless` branch. — Fix: only attach the edge when the parent binding is in an enclosing scope of the child and was declared in straight-line code before the child (or explicitly document/handle the dynamic case); add a fixture with a parent inside an uncalled function/untaken branch. — VERIFIED: traced both paths; the LSP condition at src/lsp.rs:1724-1737 compares only declaration `Location`s while the runtime lookup at src/interpreter.rs:3002-3006 requires the parent to already be in `context.objects`.

2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1573-1576 — `this` is bound to the **lexically enclosing object frame**, so an inherited method that reads an overridden field navigates to the ancestor's declaration. With `object Person` / `has age default 1` / `to can describe()` / `give back this.age` / `end` / `end`, `object Child extends Person` / `has age default 2` / `end`, `Child.describe()` runs `this.age` against the Child record (the runtime copies child entries first, src/interpreter.rs:3013-3015, so `this` sees the child's `age`), but goto on that `this.age` resolves to `Person.age` because the frame's object is `Person`. This is exactly the inheritance case the new feature claims to model. — Fix: when resolving `this` inside a method that may be inherited, prefer the nearest override on the runtime receiver (or at minimum don't claim the ancestor's field is the read's binding); add a fixture for an inherited method reading a field the child overrides. — VERIFIED: traced `this_objects`/frame binding at src/lsp.rs:1575 plus the runtime copy order at src/interpreter.rs:3013-3015; would be fully settled by running `Child.describe()` and confirming `this` is the receiver record rather than the lexical type.

